The short-envelope `{nodes: nodes}` case: `envelopeReviewThreads({nodes: nodes})` — envelopeNodesAt(['data','repository','pullRequest','reviewThreads'], data) → data.data undefined → cur null → null. Then envelopeNodesAt(['nodes'], data): cur = data.nodes → array; `cur && Array.isArray(cur.nodes)` — cur IS the array, so cur.nodes is undefined → returns null! Bug: when the holder itself IS the array (path ['nodes'] resolves to the array directly), my check `cur.nodes` is wrong.

Original code: `if (data && Array.isArray(data.nodes)) return data.nodes;` — the path lookup for ['nodes'] returns data.nodes (the array itself), and the original checked Array.isArray(rt.nodes) where rt was the HOLDER. My envelopeNodesAt treats the path result as the holder. Fix: make envelopeNodesAt check whether the resolved value is itself an array OR has a .nodes array:

```javascript
function envelopeNodesAt(path, data) {
   var cur = data;
   for (var i = 0; i < path.length; i++) {
       cur = cur ? cur[path[i]] : null;
   }
   if (Array.isArray(cur)) return cur;
   return cur && Array.isArray(cur.nodes) ? cur.nodes : null;
}
```

For the full envelope path, cur = reviewThreads object → has .nodes → returns nodes. For ['nodes'], cur = the array → returns it.

